---
// The architecture BEFORE the fix, in the same blueprint grammar as the
// pointer version later in the post: identical trunk, identical value head,
// and a policy head that is one wide fixed row of slots. The diff between the
// two blueprints is exactly one column.
---

<figure class="onb">
	<div class="spine">
		<span class="lyr in"><b>the position</b><i>78 numbers</i></span>
		<span class="wire"><i>weights: 78 × 128</i></span>
		<span class="lyr"><b>trunk layer 1</b><i>128 activations</i></span>
		<span class="wire"><i>weights: 128 × 128</i></span>
		<span class="lyr h"><b>trunk layer 2</b><i>128 activations</i><em>the trunk's read of the position</em></span>
	</div>

	<div class="split">
		<div class="side">
			<span class="slbl">value head · runs once</span>
			<span class="wire"><i>weights: 128 × 64</i></span>
			<span class="lyr sm"><b>64 activations</b></span>
			<span class="wire"><i>weights: 64 × 1</i></span>
			<span class="lyr out"><b>1 number</b><i>how this game likely ends</i></span>
		</div>
		<div class="side">
			<span class="slbl">policy head · one fixed row, ~390 slots</span>
			<span class="wire"><i>weights: 128 × ~390, one column of dials per slot</i></span>
			<span class="rowbar">
				<span class="rseg"><b>which action</b><i>4</i></span>
				<span class="rseg bad"><b>take</b><i>one slot per card</i></span>
				<span class="rseg"><b>sell</b><i>6</i></span>
				<span class="rseg"><b>count</b><i>5</i></span>
				<span class="rseg"><b>…</b><i></i></span>
			</span>
			<span class="soft">mask everything but the live question → softmax over what remains</span>
		</div>
	</div>

	<figcaption class="onb-note">
		Every slot owns its own column of dials, and a slot means whatever the codec says
		it means. The sections for every question are all always present, masked down to
		the one being asked. Keep this picture; the repair further down changes exactly
		one column of it.
	</figcaption>
</figure>

